  "blocks": [
   {
    "id": "p1",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "The effects of climate change are felt in many ways. One of them has to "
     },
     {
      "t": "blank",
      "id": "b1",
      "number": 1
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " rainfall: it will rain more on Earth, and each storm may become more "
     },
     {
      "t": "vocab",
      "v": "extreme",
      "ref": "v-extreme"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "p2",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "It doesn’t seem to "
     },
     {
      "t": "blank",
      "id": "b2",
      "number": 2
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": ", but the physics is clear. Think of rain like Earth’s sweat. When your body sweats and the moisture "
     },
     {
      "t": "gloss",
      "v": "evaporates",
      "zh": "水分蒸发"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " from your skin, it carries heat away. Likewise, water evaporating off land and oceans carries heat away from those "
     },
     {
      "t": "blank",
      "id": "b3",
      "number": 3
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": ". After moisture rises, it "
     },
     {
      "t": "gloss",
      "v": "condenses",
      "zh": "凝结"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " and falls as rain."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "p3",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "Greenhouse gases in the "
     },
     {
      "t": "vocab",
      "v": "atmosphere",
      "ref": "v-atmosphere"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " are like a blanket. They’re making it "
     },
     {
      "t": "blank",
      "id": "b4",
      "number": 4
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " for Earth to release heat into space. The more greenhouse gases it contains, the “"
     },
     {
      "t": "blank",
      "id": "b5",
      "number": 5
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "” this blanket becomes. As a result, Earth uses more "
     },
     {
      "t": "gloss",
      "v": "evaporative cooling",
      "zh": "蒸发冷却"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": ". It’s "
     },
     {
      "t": "blank",
      "id": "b6",
      "number": 6
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " to how you sweat more under a "
     },
     {
      "t": "gloss",
      "v": "duvet",
      "zh": "羽绒被"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " than a cotton sheet."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "p4",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "For scientists, it has been a challenge to understand the connection between rainfall and the "
     },
     {
      "t": "blank",
      "id": "b7",
      "number": 7
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " of climate change. Rainfall is by its nature highly complex and "
     },
     {
      "t": "blank",
      "id": "b8",
      "number": 8
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": ", says climate scientist Yoo-Geun Ham from the republic of Korea. One year might naturally be wetter or drier than the next, independent of climate change."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "p5",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "Ham and his colleagues used a deep learning model to analyse rainfall data over the past few decades. “We are having many cases of "
     },
     {
      "t": "blank",
      "id": "b9",
      "number": 9
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " downpour events,” says Ham. “We can conclude that that kind of increased "
     },
     {
      "t": "vocab",
      "v": "frequency",
      "ref": "v-frequency"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " is due to global warming.”"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "p6",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "Extreme rain becomes especially "
     },
     {
      "t": "blank",
      "id": "b10",
      "number": 10
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " when large amounts of water fall quickly. If one storm follows another, the soil might already be too wet to "
     },
     {
      "t": "blank",
      "id": "b11",
      "number": 11
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " any more water. Floods happen as a result."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "p7",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "vocab",
      "v": "Current",
      "ref": "v-current"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " infrastructure"
     },
     {
      "t": "gloss",
      "v": "",
      "zh": "基础设施"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " simply isn’t "
     },
     {
      "t": "blank",
      "id": "b12",
      "number": 12
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " for these ever-bigger floods. That will put lives at risk. Generally speaking, urban planners have designed city "
     },
     {
      "t": "gloss",
      "v": "drainage systems",
      "zh": "排水系统"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " to remove rainwater as quickly as possible to "
     },
     {
      "t": "blank",
      "id": "b13",
      "number": 13
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " flooding. But as rainfall "
     },
     {
      "t": "gloss",
      "v": "intensifies",
      "zh": "增强"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": ", "
     },
     {
      "t": "gloss",
      "v": "canals",
      "zh": "运河"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " and "
     },
     {
      "t": "gloss",
      "v": "sewers",
      "zh": "下水道"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " can’t remove the water fast enough."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "p8",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "Now one thing is "
     },
     {
      "t": "blank",
      "id": "b14",
      "number": 14
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": ": We need to turn cities into “"
     },
     {
      "t": "gloss",
      "v": "sponges",
      "zh": "海绵"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "”. That is to say, we need more green spaces so that water can seep into the ground. “We need to "
     },
     {
      "t": "blank",
      "id": "b15",
      "number": 15
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " what will happen 10 years, 20 years, and 30 years from now,” says Mohammed Ombadi, a climate scientist at the University of Michigan."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "i1",
    "type": "imageRef",
    "image": "img1"
   }
  ]
 },
